<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dong Zhuo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/192">192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dong Zhuo was a Chinese general and warlord of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Called to the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 189 during a court crisis, he seized power, deposed the young emperor in favour of Emperor Xian and ruled through terror. When a coalition of regional lords rose against him he burned Luoyang and moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 190, where he was assassinated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> in 192.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo.jpg" alt="Dong Zhuo" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dong_Zhuo_Qing_Dynasty_Illustration.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dong Zhuo, courtesy name Zhongying, was born in Lintao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longxi-commandery">Longxi Commandery</a>, in the early 140s. He was said to have been a chivalrous youth, physically strong and skilled at archery on horseback, who travelled among the Qiang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. About 165 he joined the Yulin corps of the Imperial Guard and served under Zhang Huan against a Qiang uprising. He held posts as county magistrate, inspector of Bing Province and administrator of Hedong Commandery, and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban Rebellion</a> of 184 and against rebels in Liang Province. In 188 he was named General of the Vanguard but refused the governorship of Bing Province because he did not want to leave his men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Emperor Ling of Han died in May 189, General-in-Chief He Jin ordered Dong Zhuo to bring troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> faction. He Jin was assassinated before Dong arrived. Finding his 3,000 men too few, Dong had some leave at night and re-enter at dawn so as to seem to be receiving reinforcements. He took command of He Jin's troops, won over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> and made himself Excellency of Works. That year he deposed Emperor Shao and put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xian-of-han">Liu Xie</a>, Emperor Xian, in his place, declaring himself chancellor. Rafe de Crespigny describes his conduct towards the court as bullying, oppressive and frequently bloody.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> formed a coalition of regional officials and warlords against him. Dong evacuated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and moved its people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and his troops ransacked the tombs of the Han emperors and burned the palaces. He later had the Yuan clan in Luoyang killed, and thousands of officials were wrongly accused and executed. He melted bells and bronze statues into coins of unequal weight, which devalued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> cash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dong Zhuo had sworn father and son with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a>, but the bond failed after a quarrel in which Dong threw a hand-axe at Lü. With the encouragement of the interior minister Wang Yun, Lü Bu killed him on 22 May 192 at the palace gate, after an attempt to stab him with a ji failed against his armour. His body was left in the street with a lit wick on its navel, and his kin were put to death.</p><p>His loyalists Li Jue, Guo Si, Zhang Ji and Fan Chou, refused a pardon by Wang Yun, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> with an army of about 100,000 and again held the emperor and the court. His death is seen as a destabilising event in the collapse of the Eastern Han dynasty and a precursor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dong Zhuo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Dong Zhuo</b> seized the capital in 189, the warlord Cao Cao controlled the last Han emperor from 196 CE onwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After his expulsion Yufuluo travelled to Luoyang to seek aid, but the Han court was in disorder from the clash between the Grand General He Jin and the eunuchs and the intervention of the warlord <b>Dong Zhuo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In China warlords such as <b>Dong Zhuo</b> and Cao Cao seized the central government at the end of the Han dynasty, and in Korea the general Yeon Gaesomun took control of Goguryeo in 642 after having its king killed.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It opens with corruption at court, the killing of the general He Jin by the eunuchs, and the seizure of the capital Luoyang by the warlord <b>Dong Zhuo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Encouraged by Interior Minister Wang Yun, Lü Bu, sworn to <b>Dong Zhuo</b> as a son, struck the fatal blow at the palace gate in Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-22">22 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Yuan Shu, of the powerful Yuan family, controlled the Huai River region in the chaos after the fall of <b>Dong Zhuo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhongjia">Zhongjia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Min County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.44,104.04&amp;year=182" aria-label="Min County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Dong Zhuo"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5980 4560h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5980 4560h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.44,104.04&amp;year=182">What was here in 182 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
